#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17220ac1-9331-5b65-9cb9-280c772288bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293982} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8f2890-f74d-59ee-94bd-a52d5ca0ebf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293983} {:answer/id #uuid "9f635f75-c4fb-5c76-b9ed-2e6e1c02f6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293984} {:answer/id #uuid "eeab11c1-1365-5363-961e-7eb815d63140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293985} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaf3502-3c8c-5971-84f1-5fb372470c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeab11c1-1365-5363-961e-7eb815d63140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293985} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2328d7f-6c9b-5526-af03-aca8958dcf1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72674b4f-6b99-5086-b3af-6306160b6663" :question/math-academy-id "q-88692" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)({e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
